{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Boxed warning text present for serious dermatologic reactions/HLA-B*1502 plus aplastic anemia/agranulocytosis; current source is DailyMed-based.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"Keep current boxed-warning text; replace source metadata with: FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d; effective_time=20250627; title=Carbatrol / CARBAMAZEPINE; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","rationale":"The boxed-warning content is concordant with FDA labeling, but the row's black-box source cites DailyMed, which is not permissible for this field under the audit rules.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"carbamazepine","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"carbamazepine","rationale":"FDA/Drugs@FDA labeling and NCBI LiverTox identify carbamazepine as the active ingredient/generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/"]},{"current_value":"Yes; Available in US; 1968","field":"available_in_us; filter_availability; year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Yes; Available in US; 1968","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox states carbamazepine was approved for epilepsy in the US in 1968; FDA Equetro labeling lists Initial U.S. Approval: 1968.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Carbatrol; Curatil; Epitol; Equetro; Tegretol; Tegretol Prolonged Release; Tegretol-XR","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Carbatrol; Curatil; Epitol; Equetro; Tegretol; Tegretol Prolonged Release; Tegretol-XR","rationale":"Carbatrol, Equetro, Tegretol/Tegretol XR, and Epitol are supported by FDA/NCBI sources. Curatil and Tegretol Prolonged Release were not verified from the available trusted-source evidence in this audit; no direct contradiction found, so no deletion is proposed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/postmarket-drug-safety-information-patients-and-providers/information-carbamazepine-marketed-carbatrol-equetro-tegretol-and-generics","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No distinct alternate generic name requiring a separate alias field was found; CBZ is better handled as a PubMed search alias, not an alternate generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"CBZ; Carbatrol; Tegretol; Tegretol-XR; Tegretol XR; Equetro; Epitol","rationale":"The empty alias field may miss common abbreviation/brand-title records. FDA/NCBI sources support the listed brand names; CBZ is a standard abbreviation used in PubMed-indexed carbamazepine literature.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/"]},{"current_value":"Immediate-release tablet; chewable tablet; suspension; extended-release tablet/capsule","field":"formulations_available; filter_formulation","proposed_value":"Immediate-release tablet; chewable tablet; suspension; extended-release tablet/capsule","rationale":"FDA/NCBI sources support tablets, chewable/liquid formulations, suspension, and extended-release capsule/tablet products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Mixed seizure types","field":"epilepsy_type; filter_epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Mixed seizure types","rationale":"FDA labeling indicates carbamazepine for partial/focal seizures, generalized tonic-clonic seizures, and mixed seizure patterns; it is not indicated for absence seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4, CYP1A2, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, UGT; autoinducer","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting; filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4; inducer of CYP1A2, CYP2B6, CYP2C9/19 and UGT pathways; autoinducer","rationale":"FDA labeling supports potent/strong CYP3A4 induction and induction of CYP1A2, 2B6, and 2C9/19. UGT induction and autoinduction are supported by peer-reviewed PubMed/PMC evidence; wording should avoid implying every listed pathway is classified as strong.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31650711/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14709631/","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4865535/"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism; filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA labeling states carbamazepine is primarily metabolized in the liver; LiverTox also identifies hepatic metabolism and liver injury relevance.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/"]},{"current_value":"25-65 h initially; 12-17 h after autoinduction","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"25-65 h initially; 12-17 h after autoinduction","rationale":"FDA ER labeling supports 35-40 h after a single ER dose and 12-17 h after repeated dosing; peer-reviewed/NCBI-indexed evidence supports the broader 25-65 h initial range and autoinduction explanation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4865535/"]},{"current_value":"Adults: 1200 mg/day typical labeled max; up to 1600 mg/day in selected adults","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Adults: 1200 mg/day typical labeled max; up to 1600 mg/day in selected adults","rationale":"Carbatrol labeling states adult epilepsy dosing generally should not exceed 1200 mg/day above age 15 and that doses up to 1600 mg/day have been used in adults.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Adults commonly 800 mg/day maintenance; individualized by response/tolerability","field":"minimum_effective_dose; typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Adults commonly 800 mg/day maintenance; individualized by response/tolerability","rationale":"FDA labeling gives maintenance dosing usually 800-1200 mg/day and emphasizes adjustment to minimum effective level.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 44%, CNS: drowsiness 32%, GI: nausea 29%, GI: vomiting 18%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, dermatologic: rash 7%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages; filter_symptom_category","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 44%, CNS: somnolence/drowsiness 32%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, GI: nausea 29%, GI: vomiting 18%, dermatologic: rash 7%","rationale":"The percentages match FDA Equetro pooled placebo-controlled bipolar-disorder trials. FDA term is somnolence, not drowsiness; the data are not epilepsy-trial-specific.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Antiseizure mechanism is not fully established in labeling; accepted primary proposed effect is use-dependent voltage-gated sodium-channel blockade, reducing repetitive firing and seizure spread.","field":"mechanism_of_action; filter_mechanism; mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Antiseizure mechanism is not fully established in labeling; accepted primary proposed effect is use-dependent voltage-gated sodium-channel modulation/blockade, reducing repetitive firing and seizure spread.","rationale":"FDA labeling says the mechanism remains unknown; Sills and Rogawski 2020 classifies carbamazepine among antiseizure drugs that modulate voltage-gated sodium channels. Moderate confidence is appropriate because label and mechanistic literature differ in certainty.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063)","rationale":"DailyMed should be replaced with FDA/accessdata for a trusted FDA label citation; the Sills/Rogawski PubMed record supports the sodium-channel mechanism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label + peer-reviewed review","rationale":"The FDA label supports mechanism uncertainty but not the full sodium-channel wording; the mechanistic classification relies on peer-reviewed review evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"No typical QT prolongation; can cause conduction abnormalities/AV block and QRS effects in toxicity","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No FDA-label evidence of typical QT prolongation; conduction/arrhythmia caution including reported AV heart block and overdose conduction disorders.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports AV heart block and overdose conduction disorders. The current QRS-specific wording and broad 'no typical QT prolongation' statement were not directly supported by the cited row sources.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution; QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution","rationale":"The row's narrative QT field says no typical QT prolongation, and FDA labeling supports conduction/AV-block caution rather than a QT-prolongation classification.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Current ASM; also used for trigeminal neuralgia and bipolar disorder/mania in some markets.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"Current ASM; also used for trigeminal neuralgia and bipolar disorder/mania in some markets.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports epilepsy, trigeminal neuralgia, and Equetro acute manic/mixed episodes associated with bipolar I disorder.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA labeling; FDA Drugs@FDA/accessdata labeling; NCBI LiverTox; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063); PubMed RCT metadata (PMID 15156070); NCBI Bookshelf NICE epilepsy evidence review","rationale":"The named Epilepsy Society and Epilepsy Foundation Australia sources are outside the requested trusted domains, and DailyMed is not acceptable for black-box verification. The proposed sources are trusted and map to the checked facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]}],"generic_name":"carbamazepine","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The retained Sobaniec 2004 record is a carbamazepine placebo-controlled clinical trial, but NCBI/NICE evidence review marks it as having no outcome of interest for add-on therapy; no RR50 differential was verified.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"No extractable drug-minus-placebo median percent change outcome was verified from the retained PubMed RCT/outcome evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"No extractable drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential was verified from the retained RCT/outcome evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose; plot_diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose; plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"","rationale":"Plot fields are appropriately blank because corresponding differential outcome values are NR/not extractable.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d; published=Jul 03, 2025; title=CARBATROL (CARBAMAZEPINE) CAPSULE, EXTENDED RELEASE [TAKEDA PHARMACEUTICALS AMERICA, INC.]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d; effective_time=20250627; title=Carbatrol / CARBAMAZEPINE; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","reason":"DailyMed is not permissible for black-box warning verification; FDA/openFDA is permissible.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1"]},{"current_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4, CYP1A2, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, UGT; autoinducer","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4; inducer of CYP1A2, CYP2B6, CYP2C9/19 and UGT pathways; autoinducer","reason":"FDA label specifically calls CYP3A4 strong/potent and lists CYP1A2, 2B6, 2C9/19 induction; UGT support comes from PubMed evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31650711/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14709631/"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 44%, CNS: drowsiness 32%, GI: nausea 29%, GI: vomiting 18%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, dermatologic: rash 7%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 44%, CNS: somnolence/drowsiness 32%, neurologic: ataxia 15%, GI: nausea 29%, GI: vomiting 18%, dermatologic: rash 7%","reason":"Uses the FDA label term somnolence and keeps the same verified percentages.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063)","reason":"Replaces DailyMed wording with FDA/accessdata and adds PMID for the peer-reviewed mechanism source.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label + peer-reviewed review","reason":"The sodium-channel mechanism is supported by peer-reviewed review evidence; FDA label mainly supports uncertainty.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"No typical QT prolongation; can cause conduction abnormalities/AV block and QRS effects in toxicity","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No FDA-label evidence of typical QT prolongation; conduction/arrhythmia caution including reported AV heart block and overdose conduction disorders.","reason":"Aligns the field with FDA label-supported conduction warnings and avoids unsupported QRS-specific wording.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution; QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution","reason":"The QT-prolongation filter conflicts with the row narrative and was not supported by the FDA label evidence used in this audit.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"CBZ; Carbatrol; Tegretol; Tegretol-XR; Tegretol XR; Equetro; Epitol","reason":"Adds supported aliases/brand names likely to improve PubMed retrieval without creating duplicate drug rows.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA labeling; FDA Drugs@FDA/accessdata labeling; NCBI LiverTox; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063); PubMed RCT metadata (PMID 15156070); NCBI Bookshelf NICE epilepsy evidence review","reason":"Uses trusted domains and sources that actually support the populated cells audited here.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Sobaniec2004","pmid":"15156070","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed-linked metadata and NCBI/NICE citation identify a carbamazepine/Carbatrol placebo-controlled, double-blind clinical trial in refractory epilepsy. Outcome differentials remain NR because the NICE evidence review marks it as no outcome of interest for the extracted efficacy endpoints.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/"},{"label":"Hessen2010 proposed by deterministic update","pmid":"20955400","proposed_action":"do_not_add","rationale":"This is a randomized double-blind withdrawal/neuropsychological study in well-controlled epilepsy; the abstract states medication choice was not randomized to placebo, and it is not a phase II/III placebo-controlled carbamazepine seizure-efficacy RCT for RR50/MPC/seizure-freedom extraction.","status":"not_phase_ii_iii","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20955400/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d; published=Jul 03, 2025; title=CARBATROL (CARBAMAZEPINE) CAPSULE, EXTENDED RELEASE [TAKEDA PHARMACEUTICALS AMERICA, INC.]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d; effective_time=20250627; title=Carbatrol / CARBAMAZEPINE; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","reason":"Black-box field must use FDA/openFDA/accessdata, not DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063)","reason":"Clarifies trusted source and PubMed identifier.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA labeling; FDA Drugs@FDA/accessdata labeling; NCBI LiverTox; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review (PMID 32120063); PubMed RCT metadata (PMID 15156070); NCBI Bookshelf NICE epilepsy evidence review","reason":"Replaces non-trusted/overbroad named sources with trusted sources that support the row facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id:%22bc03e499-5bac-4293-bff4-6864153a624d%22&limit=1","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/020712s038lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/021710s018lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548097/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32120063/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15156070/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/niceng217er26/"]}],"summary":"Carbamazepine row is broadly correct for identity, US availability, indications, formulations, dosing, metabolism, adverse-event percentages, mechanism, and NR outcome fields. Updates are needed for FDA black-box source metadata, evidence source hygiene, PubMed aliases, enzyme wording, adverse-event terminology, mechanism source tier, and QT/conduction filter wording. Do not add Hessen2010 as a qualifying efficacy RCT; do not add 'Carbamazepine' as a trade name."}
